aloha-oe -- notes on the score and synthesizer

The score
---------

Aloha 'Oe was composed by Queen Liliuokalani.  The melody in
src/melody.h follows the upper vocal line of J. Bodewalt Lampe's
1912 arrangement, with the W. A. Quincke edition as a second source.
Both printed scores are in A-flat major, in common time, marked
Moderato.  Neither gives a numerical metronome marking.

The program transposes the melody to E major.  One tick is a sixteenth
note; four ticks make one notated quarter note.  The verse, including
its pickup, occupies 130 ticks; the chorus occupies 126.  Together they
make sixteen bars of 4/4.  The verse rest and the chorus pickup share
a bar.  Padding each section to eight complete bars would spoil this
join.  Sample positions are calculated from absolute score time, so
rounding errors do not accumulate from one note to the next.

Ties, dotted notes, rests and sixteenth notes are retained.  The
optional turn in the third verse bar is omitted.  The last chorus
note is Lampe's dotted half under a fermata, played for its written
duration.  Quincke instead has a half note followed by a quarter rest.
The printed fermata does not prescribe an additional holding time.

The OpenScore Lieder transcription of the Quincke edition provides
a machine-readable source for the melody.  Lampe ties several verse
notes which Quincke repeats; the final chorus note also differs as
described above.  The last eighth note of chorus bar 7 is G4 in the
printed key, or D-sharp4 in the program's key.

The score is compiled into the program.  No XML or MIDI reader is
needed to build or run it.  Integer pitch numbers in the source are
an equal-tempered notation, with A4 at 440 Hz.

Arrangement
-----------

Johnny Cash's recording on American VI: Ain't No Grave supplied the
reference key, pace and broad arrangement.  The default tempo is 49
notated quarter notes per minute, giving 98 eighth notes per minute.
The --bpm option always counts quarters; setting it to 98 doubles
the speed.  The recorded accompaniment was estimated at roughly
98-99 eighth notes per minute over the main sections.

At the default tempo the arrangement runs as follows.  Times include
the pickups and refer to the generated performance.

        Introduction                 0.000 s
        Verse                        9.796 s
        First chorus                49.592 s
        Instrumental chorus         88.776 s
        Second chorus              127.959 s
        Coda                       167.755 s

There are 145 notated quarter notes, followed by about 2.249 seconds
of decay, for a total of 179.800 seconds.  E, A and B7 supply the main
accompaniment.  These section timings do not imply a note-for-note
transcription of the recording or a reproduction of its free tempo.

David R. Ferguson's account of the session identifies Pat McLaughlin
on acoustic guitar and Jack Clement on slide guitar, with some guitar
by Cash.  He describes a quiet, close vocal and an album without drums.
The arrangement here uses a soft strummed part on the left, picked
eighth notes on the right, and slide guitar between the sung phrases.
The quiet bass part is an addition; the account does not assign bass
to this particular track.  Piano and percussion are optional and
are silent at their default gains.

The introduction includes descending G-sharp to F-sharp slides and
short upward slides to A and G-sharp.  A quieter descending response
appears in the instrumental chorus.  Their timing and the connecting
notes are approximate; this is an arrangement, not an exact
transcription of the recorded slide part.  No part of the recording
is included in or read by the program.

Synthesis
---------

The renderer adds damped harmonic oscillators.  Each partial has its
own decay.  Smooth attacks and releases avoid discontinuities, and
partials are cut off below the Nyquist frequency.  Guitar uses up to
64 partials, with a simple body resonance and pick noise.  The steel
part uses a softer attack, vibrato and slides.  A slide integrates a
smooth frequency curve, preserving phase and arriving at the scored
destination pitch.
Short delays and feedback provide the room sound.

Some timbre settings were compared with measurements of the recording.
Held steel notes there carry a vibrato of roughly 23 cents either way
at about 5.6 Hz, and fade by about 12 dB per second; the steel part
uses that vibrato.  Its notes fade quickly at first and then leave a
quieter, slower tail, so that the steel still sustains under the
other parts.  Its level matches the earlier, slower steel.  The guitar
partial count and pick noise were raised until the long-term spectrum
of the introduction and instrumental chorus was within about 4 dB of
the recording in each octave from 80 Hz to 5 kHz.  The B7 bass root
is B2, not B1.  The recording sits about 5 cents sharp of A4 = 440 Hz;
the program keeps standard tuning.

Direct harmonics make the pitch easy to set and check.  A
Karplus-Strong string would need compensation for the phase delay
of its loop filter.  The present model does not attempt a complete
physical model of a guitar, its body, or the recording room.

The singing voice uses a band-limited derivative of an analytic
glottal pulse, three moving formants and a low-frequency branch.
Breath, irregular voicing and a small phoneme planner supply the
remaining articulation.  The source, timing rules and tract targets
are approximations; they were not measured from Cash's vocal tract.
No recorded phonemes, sampled instruments or trained voice model
are used.

Each sung note has an IPA syllable.  The planner reserves short
intervals for consonants and gives most of the note to its vowel.
Diphthongs, nasals, liquids, stops and fricatives have separate
targets.  Noise level is independent of voicing.  A fricative fades
at its own band frequency on entering a vowel, rather than sweeping
that band down through the vowel.  This keeps the consonants from
overwhelming the sustained voice.

The retro voice applies a 3400 Hz low-pass filter, approximately
11025 Hz sample-and-hold, and 127 signed quantization levels to the
generated vocal part.  Accompaniment retains its usual precision.
This is a deliberately coarse speech sound, not an emulation of
the NES audio hardware.  In particular, the NES DMC plays sample
data; it does not generate speech from IPA.

Pronunciation remains approximate, and the voice only suggests a
low, rough male singer.  The IPA option auditions supplied phonemes;
it is not a general text-to-speech system.

IPA input
---------

The --ipa argument is UTF-8.  These are the supported speech sounds:

        Vowels:       a ɑ æ ɛ e ɪ i ɔ o ʊ u ə ʌ ɜ
        Stops:        p b t d k g ɡ ʔ
        Fricatives:   s z f v θ ð ʃ ʒ h
        Nasals:       m n ŋ
        Approximants: l r ɹ w j

The letters g and ɡ select the same sound, as do r and ɹ.  The r sound
is an approximant, not a trill.  Consecutive vowels form a transition;
the first of a vowel pair receives most of the available sustain.
There are no recorded diphthongs or affricates.

Primary stress ˈ (U+02C8) and secondary stress ˌ (U+02CC) increase
the share of time assigned to the next vowel.  Length ː (U+02D0),
half-length ˑ (U+02D1), and ASCII colon increase the preceding
vowel's share.  These marks divide the existing note duration;
they do not extend the note or change its pitch.

Spaces introduce short pauses.  Tabs, newlines, carriage returns
and non-breaking spaces are treated as spaces.  Repeated, leading
and trailing spaces do not add extra pauses.  These separators
and punctuation marks are accepted but do not produce a sound:

        . , - ' ( ) [ ] /
        U+2019       right single quotation mark
        U+0361       combining double inverted breve (upper tie)
        U+035C       combining double breve below (lower tie)

Other symbols and malformed UTF-8 are rejected.  The limit is 96
segments, counting internal pauses.  The planner accepts consonant
sequences, but useful singing examples normally include a vowel.
The manual page gives the audition options and defaults.

Rendering and lyric timing
-------------------------

The complete passage is rendered as interleaved stereo floating-point
PCM before playback.  The default full song at 48 kHz requires about
69 MB for this buffer.  Volume selects the rendered peak, rather than
changing the system mixer.  A mix and its exported parts share the
same master gain.  Each part retains its relative level, room sound
and excerpt fades; a disabled part is exported as silence.

An offset keeps earlier note envelopes and reverberation before
cutting the requested excerpt.  The score export describes the full
selected section even when the audio excerpt is shortened.

The 71 lyric words, divided into 109 sung syllables, use the same
score clock as the notes.  A syllable's letters are written to
standard output one by one from its onset, over at most half its sung
length and no more than 0.08 seconds apart, and output is flushed after
each letter.  A syllable held over two notes is printed once.  On a
colour terminal each phrase is drawn in grey up to two seconds before
it is sung, then redrawn with its sung letters highlighted; elsewhere
unsung text is never shown.  Phrases end with a newline.  Gaps of four
seconds or more without singing print an instrumental marker, letter
by letter across the gap, completing two seconds before the next
phrase begins.  An excerpt beginning inside a word or gap first shows
what of it has already passed.
Playback progress comes from the selected audio driver and is polled
by the calling thread, not printed from an audio callback.  Driver
buffering limits the accuracy: NetBSD reports channel-buffer progress,
and Windows may fall back to completed buffers if no position is
available.  Pure file rendering has no real-time lyric output.

Checks
------

make check runs the built-in score and synthesis checks.  They cover
the score grid and duration, sample rounding, rendered guitar/bass/
piano pitch, and full ensemble length, peak and finite samples.
Slides and voice deliberately vary their instantaneous pitch.

These checks do not assess the intelligibility of the voice or the
resemblance to the recording.  Build and playback have been tested
on macOS.  The other audio back ends have not been tested on their
target systems.  See INSTALL for device requirements and port-specific
restrictions.
